• Constructors: Special methods with same name as class and NO return type. Automatically invoked by new to initialize objects.
• Uses of 'this':
  1. Resolving shadowed instance variables: this.name = name;
  2. Constructor Chaining: this(args); (MUST be the absolute first statement in constructor body).
  3. Passing current object as argument: process(this);
  4. Returning current object for method chaining: return this;
  5. Invoking current class method: this.display();

1. final Variable: Value becomes constant; cannot be reassigned once initialized: final double PI = 3.14159;
2. final Method: Cannot be overridden by any subclass, protecting core logic from modification.
3. final Class: Cannot be inherited / extended by any class: final class String { ... } (used for immutability and security).

• Uses of 'super':
  1. Calling superclass constructor: super(args); (MUST be first statement in subclass constructor).
  2. Calling overridden superclass method: super.show();
  3. Accessing shadowed superclass field: super.data;
• Constructor Calling Order: In an inheritance hierarchy, constructors execute strictly from Base Class down to Derived Subclass (Grandparent → Parent → Child) because each constructor automatically calls super() before its own body executes.

• Dynamic Method Dispatch: Mechanism by which a call to an overridden method is resolved dynamically at RUNTIME rather than compile-time.
• Principle: A superclass reference variable can hold a reference to any subclass object:
  SuperClass ref = new SubClass();
  ref.display();
• Execution: The JVM determines which implementation of display() to execute based on the TYPE OF THE ACTUAL OBJECT referenced in Heap memory, NOT the reference type.
• Achieves true runtime polymorphism and loose coupling.

• Subclass / Specialized exception handlers MUST PRECEDE superclass / General exception handlers.
• Incorrect:
  catch (Exception e) { ... }
  catch (ArithmeticException e) { ... } // COMPILE ERROR: Unreachable code!
• Correct:
  catch (ArithmeticException e) { ... }
  catch (Exception e) { ... }

• Purpose: Ensures a class has EXACTLY ONE instance throughout application lifecycle, providing a global access point.
• Requirements: private constructor, private static instance, public static getInstance().
• Thread-Safe Double-Checked Locking:
```java
public class DBConn {
    private static volatile DBConn instance;
    private DBConn() {}
    public static DBConn getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (DBConn.class) {
                if (instance == null) instance = new DBConn();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

1. Load/Register Driver: Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
2. Establish Connection: Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);
3. Create Statement: PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM Users WHERE id=?");
4. Execute Query/Update:
   - ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery(); (for SELECT)
   - int rows = ps.executeUpdate(); (for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE)
5. Process Results & Close: while(rs.next()) { ... } rs.close(); ps.close(); con.close();

